---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import Specimen from '../components/Specimen.astro';
import { systems, getSystem } from '../data/systems';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Layout title="Our approach — Ancient Numbers" active="about">
  <div class="container">
    <article class="about-page">
      <span class="eyebrow">CURIOSITY, WITH CARE</span>
      <h1>
        A human story,
        <br />
        <em>carefully drawn.</em>
      </h1>
      <p class="about-intro">
        Numbers are a shared idea. The ways we express them are wonderfully particular. Ancient
        Numbers is an invitation to look closer.
      </p>
      <h2>One collection. Many traditions.</h2>
      <p>
        This project began with a simple question: what would an everyday number look like in a
        different system? The answer reaches beyond written digits. Our collection includes number
        words, knots, gestures, ancient inscriptions, and recent Indigenous inventions. The name
        Ancient Numbers is a starting point, not a claim that every tradition here is ancient.
      </p>
      <h2>A considered visual language.</h2>
      <p>
        Every displayed numeral is a vector drawing from our shared artwork collection. Optical
        scale, spacing, ink weight, and presentation connect the forms across the interface.
        Meaningful features—stroke counts, wedges, curves, enclosed spaces, and placement—stay
        distinct. A coherent theme should help you see a tradition, not erase its character.
      </p>
      <div class="style-proof">
        {['kaktovik', 'maya', 'cistercian', 'rods'].map((id) => (
          <div>
            <Specimen
              system={getSystem(id)!}
              value={id === 'cistercian' ? 2026 : id === 'rods' ? 7 : 12}
              compact
            />
            <small>{getSystem(id)!.name}</small>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        Geometric forms are custom drawn. Where intricate cursive shapes need a reliable reference,
        openly licensed font outlines are optically harmonized and credited in the glyph manifest.
        The collection combines original drawing and attributed adaptation; it does not claim that
        all historical forms were invented here. Downloads contain vector paths and need no fonts.
      </p>
      <h2>Accuracy includes knowing the limits.</h2>
      <p>
        Each entry names a specific tradition, variant, source, and supported range. General
        converters follow documented rules; specimen explorers present individually verified
        examples. Some implementation ranges are deliberately narrow. We distinguish those app
        limits from a historical tradition’s capabilities.
      </p>
      <p>
        Labels, cell boundaries, place values, and schematic diagrams are modern reading aids. They
        are identified as such. Spoken traditions receive authentic words, and physical counting
        methods receive diagrams. Neither is assigned an invented ancient numeral alphabet.
      </p>
      <h2>Arithmetic, without shortcuts.</h2>
      <p>
        Calculation uses exact rational arithmetic in your browser. Finite decimals and fractions
        retain their exact values. When a selected renderer cannot express a result, the
        mathematical answer stays visible with an explanation. Displaying an operation in a
        tradition’s notation does not claim to reproduce its historical calculation method.
      </p>
      <h2>Free, open, and local.</h2>
      <p>
        The entire app is static and runs in your browser. There are no accounts, runtime APIs,
        tracking scripts, or paid services. The project is published on GitHub Pages, with its
        original code and artwork available under the MIT license. Adapted artwork retains the
        applicable Open Font License and attribution.
      </p>
      <a class="inline-link" href="https://github.com/CumputerSluts/ancient-numbers">
        Explore the repository <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <h2 id="sources">Follow the evidence.</h2>
      <p>
        Community educational publications, museum material, specialist research, and Unicode
        documentation inform the collection. The individual pages explain which reference supports
        the selected notation. Historical terminology and dates are presented with their source
        context.
      </p>
      <ul class="about-source-index">
        {[...systems]
          .sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name))
          .map((s) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`${base}systems/${s.id}/`}>{s.name} ↗</a>
              <small>{s.sources[0].title}</small>
            </li>
          ))}
      </ul>
      <h2>A collection that can grow.</h2>
      <p>
        Corrections are welcome. A useful contribution includes a specific example, a documented
        variant, and a source someone else can inspect. New systems need verified notation rules or
        clearly bounded specimens, artwork references, tests, and license information.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
